## MUTATION-HARNESS-MTIME-RESTORE - REGISTERED
{
 "id": "MUTATION-HARNESS-MTIME-RESTORE",
 "title": "A mutation harness that restores with shutil.copy2 leaves the mutant linked into every later build",
 "why": "Measured in wave 35 by card MUTEX-SHARE-CROSS-ARMS, in its own harness. shutil.copy2 PRESERVES the backup's mtime, so a restored source file is OLDER than the fingerprint cargo recorded while building the mutant: cargo considers the crate up to date, skips the rebuild, and every later cargo invocation in that worktree silently links the MUTATED crate. This is not theoretical - the first full workspace run after that card's harness reported two failures, ct03_proof_leg's ct03_artifact_is_the_lean_proved_shared_reads_shape (Q500, 'the derived transferability judgment and the capture enumeration disagree about this capture') and ct_corpus's stage-agreement row, which were the arm-d-second-sharing-kind mutation still linked into qcore-core and NOT a defect in the tree. The working tree was pristine against HEAD throughout, which is what makes the mode dangerous: git status cannot see it, and the same mistake in the DISCHARGING direction produces a false GREEN rather than a false red. That card's harness now restores by rewriting the file and asserts the round-trip, and both tests were re-run green before its gate. WHAT IS NOT DONE: the hazard is generic to every mutation harness in the tree - docs/reports/c-gateway-return-call-w4/scripts/mutations.py is the other one and was NOT audited for this pattern - and the rule is not yet in CLAUDE.md. Discharge: read the other harness, and land the proposed CLAUDE.md measurement-hygiene bullet quoted in docs/reports/mutex-share-cross-arms/README.md section 11.",
 "source": "docs/reports/mutex-share-cross-arms/README.md",
 "recorded_by": "MUTEX-SHARE-CROSS-ARMS"
}
